INT6 receive in a static kernel (-DZ3660ETH_STATIC_INTR); drain again after re-enabling

The module's interrupt path (int6, int2, rx_work, the ring resync) is now
also built into a static kernel with -DZ3660ETH_STATIC_INTR.  There is no
intr_attach: the 040/060 port's relink-040-z3660intr.sh points p6int's
call at z3660eth_p6 and an int2_tbl slot at z3660eth_p2, which call the
handler that was there first (z3660eth_p6_next, z3660eth_p2_next, set by
the relink).  The switch is z3660eth_intr in /dev/kmem, looked at in
every poll tick: 6 runs the level-6 self-test and enables INT6 receive,
0 turns it off; the poll tick drains in both modes.  Without the define
the static driver is byte for byte as before (.text and .data compared).

Both forms: after re-enabling the board's interrupt, rx_work drains once
more.  The firmware raises INT6 at once only for the first frame in an
empty ring (rxkick) and for frames left after a strobe (reraise), both
only while the interrupt is on; frames that came during the masked drain
waited for the nag, about 60 a second.  Measured in pvmk net -i on the
A3000: 60 -> 170-200 interrupts a second at 1000-2000 frames/s.

